import { quickstart } from './quickstart.js';
import { delegation } from './delegation.js';
import { offchain } from './offchain.js';
const code = (text, language = 'typescript') => ({ code: text, language });
export const pages = [
  {
    id: 'overview', group: 'START HERE', title: 'Introduction', eyebrow: 'MEET PILOT',
    description: 'Approve a bounded session once. Your pilot takes over signing, so you can keep moving.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'what-is-pilot', title: 'A little less friction.', html: '<p>Pilot takes over the signing work you’ve authorized. Approve a bounded session with your wallet, then let its session key sign allowed actions while you use the app. You stay in control of the permissions and budget.</p><p>The session SDK separates local acceptance from chain confirmation. Your app prepares actions; Pilot checks the allowance, saves the action, updates projected state, and lets an adapter sign and settle it in the background.</p>' },
      { id: 'how-it-works', title: 'One approval. A smoother flight.', flow: true, html: '<p>For on-chain actions, the owner authorizes a restricted session by funding a compatible CKB cell. Each accepted move settles as its own transaction. For <a href="#offchain-signing">off-chain actions</a>, the owner signs a scoped message delegation; the receiving app verifies each session-signed action without a CKB transaction or fee.</p>' },
      { id: 'end-to-end', title: 'From connection to recovery', html: '<p>Connect an existing owner wallet through Pilot, create or resume a username, review the manifest, and select the supported action. A new username needs a separate ownership signature. The app saves a session key, then the owner signs the session-funding transaction. Automatic actions become available after funding confirms.</p><p>The browser demo funds <strong>150.02 test CKB</strong>, plus a setup fee: 150 CKB of protected, recoverable cell capacity and 0.02 CKB for 20 moves at 0.001 CKB each. These are demo settings. The funds stay in the user’s contract-restricted session cell; they are not deposited into a pooled Owl account.</p><p>Pilot prepares up to ten alternative next actions, checks permissions and budget, reserves a ready action’s fee, and saves it before returning optimistic state. The app renders immediately. In the background, the adapter signs with the session key, saves exact transaction bytes, broadcasts, and waits for CKB confirmation. Each move consumes the current cell and creates its successor. After three confirmed demo moves, 150.017 CKB remains.</p><p>Uncertain submissions keep their queue and reservation and reconcile the same signed transaction. Definitive failures roll back failed and dependent actions. After pending settlement is resolved, an owner-signed recovery returns protected capacity and unused budget. Recovery needs an ordinary owner-wallet cell and a separate fee; consuming the session cell revokes its delegated authority.</p><p>The original public deployment charges user fees. Separate sponsored policy variants and a pool-signing service are implemented; enabling them requires new deployed code cells, a funded pool, and the server-only pool key. On-chain expiry is not implemented. Username transfers still require owner signatures. The <a href="https://github.com/Birdmannn/owl#end-to-end-what-happens-behind-the-scenes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">README’s end-to-end guide</a> illustrates funding, background settlement, and recovery with Mermaid diagrams.</p>' },
      { id: 'first-interaction', title: 'Your first interaction', html: '<p>Prepare a move, check its readiness, and render the optimistic state after acceptance. Prepare the next possibilities again whenever state changes.</p>', ...code("await pilot.prepare([{\n  id: 'forward',\n  action: { steps: 1 },\n}]);\n\nif (pilot.readiness('forward').ready) {\n  await pilot.execute('forward');\n  render(pilot.state);\n  await pilot.prepare(nextMoves(pilot.state));\n}") },
      { id: 'developer-projects-proposal', title: 'Proposed V2: developer projects', html: '<p>The proposed next layer lets a developer create a Pilot project, publish its manifest and limits, and integrate with a public project ID. An optional server-only project secret would authorize privileged management and short-lived access for backend-approved sponsorship. The public ID identifies configuration; it does not prove that an HTTP caller is the SDK or replace wallet consent. Browser origin allowlists remain an additional browser control, not authentication.</p><p>This design is <strong>proposed, not implemented</strong>. Project dashboards, scoped secrets/tokens, project accounting, createPilotClient and PilotProvider are not current SDK features. Existing PilotModal and PilotAccountsProvider integrations remain the available APIs. Read <a href="https://github.com/Birdmannn/owl/blob/main/IMPLEMENTATION_V2.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IMPLEMENTATION_V2.md</a> for the proposed API, public versus backend-authorized sponsorship, security boundaries, migration plan and acceptance criteria. The owner still approves selected permissions, and on-chain rules still restrict the session key.</p>' },
      { id: 'available-today', title: 'What’s ready today', table: [['Optimistic engine', 'Prevalidation, budget reservations, durable acceptance, sequential settlement.'], ['CKB policies', 'Public counter session plus a locally verified reusable delegation lock for selected u64 transitions.'], ['Pilot connector & modal', 'Existing-wallet wrapper, verified shared usernames, balance, manifest inspection, explicit permission selection.'], ['Browser demo', 'Real testnet moves, reload recovery, live transaction queue, detailed logs and receipts.'], ['Off-chain signing', 'Scoped owner grants, session-signed likes/follows/posts, receiving-app verification, replay protection, and revocation.']], html: '<p>Start with the <a href="#wallet-flow">Pilot wallet flow</a> to see what users approve and how apps integrate it.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Early, and intentionally bounded.</strong><p>Pilot is a testnet prototype. The public testnet flight uses a fixed counter; a new reusable delegation policy supports selected bounded u64 transitions and is verified on local CKB. Expiration is local only; sponsored variants need deployment and a configured fee-signing service; general app policies and on-chain expiration are not implemented.</p></div>' },
    ],
  },
  quickstart,
  delegation,
  offchain,
  {
    id: 'security', group: 'CORE CONCEPTS', title: 'Security & identity', eyebrow: 'WHO IS PILOT ACTING FOR?',
    description: 'What binds an action to its owner, what the checks protect, and what remains unsafe.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'owner-binding', title: 'Authority comes from signatures and cell policy', html: '<p>A username and an address-named browser entry are not authorization. On-chain, the delegated lock binds the owner lock hash, session public key, selected rules, app type, fees, and protected capacity. Session signatures bind the exact transaction and, for reusable delegation, the action and every witness. The contract enforces the bounded transition.</p><p>Off-chain, the owner signs a grant binding their verified signing identity and CKB address to a session key, manifest, exact app origin, permission subset, and expiry. Each signed action binds the complete grant content hash, target, payload, nonce, and validity period. The receiver must use <code>acceptOffchainAction</code> with atomic replay/revocation enforcement; cryptographic verification alone does not prevent replay.</p><p>When restoring a session, compare it to the currently connected wallet. Use <code>PilotOffchainSession.create(authorization, manifest, privateKey, Date.now, connectedAddress)</code> for the expected-owner check. The demo checks saved on-chain owner locks and off-chain owners before using or retrying them.</p>' },
      { id: 'owner-lock-restrictions', title: 'Only signature-required owner locks', html: '<p>Owner recovery currently proves consent by including an input under the configured owner lock. This requires a lock that authenticates every spend. Anyone-Can-Pay and other permissionless/optional modes do not meet that assumption.</p><p>New session setup rejects unknown owner scripts and unsupported modes. Supported normal configurations include secp256k1, OmniLock with recognized signature identities and flags zero, primary JoyID, normal Nostr, and the recognized PW lock configuration. This restriction applies to on-chain session ownership, not username registration or independently verified message grants.</p><div class="callout"><strong>The contract assumption remains.</strong><p>The guard does not repair existing cells funded with permissive owner locks or protect transactions constructed outside the SDK. Do not fund those configurations. Recover existing unsafe cells; a full contract repair requires a new policy and deployment.</p></div>' },
      { id: 'signing-review', title: 'Independent transaction review', html: '<p>Builders receive cloned transaction/cell objects so they cannot rewrite the expected validation baseline. Reviewed owner operations reject unapproved cells, assets, outputs, state changes, and excess fees. Signing-only results must preserve the reviewed raw transaction and app type witnesses. Owner receipt reconciliation uses the same validated snapshot, preventing a second storage read from replacing the transaction after its account check. Counter owner setup/recovery use a 0.01 CKB fee ceiling.</p><p>Counter setup offers <code>onSigned(cell, bytes)</code> before broadcast. Persist both atomically and retry those exact bytes after an uncertain response; the demo does this. The reusable delegation owner path requires a durable receipt store. The legacy counter recovery helper still lacks that mandatory receipt-store protocol; interrupted recovery may need manual chain reconciliation.</p>' },
      { id: 'remaining-signing-risks', title: 'Current risks before production', html: '<p>The demo keeps raw session keys in localStorage, accessible to same-origin JavaScript. A compromised app or dependency can invoke approved actions and consume the permitted budget. The modal is not an isolated key vault. On-chain session expiry remains local only: a copied key retains its contract allowance until recovery or exhaustion. RPC responses and configured script metadata are trusted.</p><p>This code review added adversarial regression tests and fixed verified issues; it is not independent OpenAI certification or formal verification. The project remains a bounded testnet prototype. Read the <a href="https://github.com/Birdmannn/owl/blob/main/SECURITY_AUDIT.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">security review and open findings</a> for the threat model, fixes, dependency advisory, evidence, and remaining limits.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'wallet-flow', group: 'CORE CONCEPTS', title: 'Wallet connection & consent', eyebrow: 'SIGN THESE ON MY BEHALF',
    description: 'Pilot wraps an existing owner wallet. Users choose which supported actions its session key may sign.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'connect', title: 'Connect through Pilot', table: [['1. Choose Pilot', 'Connect wallet opens the app’s wallet picker with Pilot as an option.'], ['2. Connect the owner', 'Pilot opens CCC to connect an existing wallet. The owner private key stays in that wallet.'], ['3. Create or return to a profile', 'Register a unique username with a wallet signature, or continue the name already registered for this wallet.'], ['4. Review the account', 'See the owner’s CKB testnet address and balance; copy the address or refresh the balance.'], ['5. Choose permissions', 'Inspect the app manifest, select Fly forward, and review fees, budget, and protected capacity.'], ['6. Authorize once', 'The owner signs the session-funding transaction. Once confirmed, Pilot signs approved moves with its session key.']], html: '<p>The permission checkbox starts unchecked. Closing the modal or connecting a wallet alone grants no automatic signing authority.</p>', demo: true },
      { id: 'manifest-consent', title: 'Bind consent to the manifest', html: '<p>The legacy counter manifest describes the app, supported permission IDs, deployment identity, per-move fee, total budget, and protected capacity. For version-2 developer manifests, see <a href="#cell-delegation">cell delegation</a>. The resulting <code>PilotGrant</code> records the selected permission and a hash of the manifest.</p><p>The legacy connector recognizes the deployed <code>counter-v1</code> script and rejects unknown scripts, unknown permissions, missing selections, and duplicate selections. The app checks the grant before execution. Changed manifests need fresh approval; revoke existing sessions before shipping an incompatible manifest.</p><p>On-chain authority comes from the counter contract, which enforces the session signature, increment-by-one transition, transaction shape, fee limits, budget debit, and protected capacity. Manifest labels alone do not grant authority.</p>' },
      { id: 'profile-scope', title: 'An existing wallet, a shared username', html: '<p>Pilot appears as a wallet option inside integrating apps. This version does not create a new owner wallet or install a globally discoverable wallet provider.</p><p>Pilot binds each username to one verified wallet identity and CKB recipient address. JoyID names bind to a primary P-256 passkey; other supported wallets can register with their signing identity. Registration requires a signed challenge, separate from session approval. See <a href="#usernames">Usernames &amp; transfers</a> for username and transfer behavior.</p><p>The demo still stores its session key in localStorage; a production session-key vault is not implemented.</p>' },
      { id: 'account-switching', title: 'Multiple accounts in one browser', html: '<p>With <code>PilotAccountsProvider</code>, both Pilot modals show <strong>Accounts in this browser</strong>. The list remembers previously connected owners across wallets, including multiple users from the same wallet. It is scoped to the current site and network; it cannot enumerate every wallet account or passkey that has never connected. One owner is active at a time.</p><p>Choose an account to reuse a live connection after checking its address and signing identity. If no reusable connection exists, Pilot reopens wallet authentication and checks that the wallet returned the requested account. A different account cannot silently activate the requested account’s saved permissions. The account list contains public connection hints, not private keys or authority. Profiles, balances, app sessions, permissions, queues, and activity follow the selected owner; switching does not transfer or revoke another owner’s grants.</p><p><strong>Add account / choose wallet</strong> restarts the CCC connection flow behind the scenes. <strong>Add JoyID account</strong> creates a fresh JoyID signer and opens its authentication popup even when another JoyID is connected. The installed JoyID SDK does not expose a documented force-registration flag. Choose another JoyID or create a new account if its wallet UI offers that option; Pilot cannot clear JoyID’s cross-origin login state or force a new passkey prompt. Adding another passkey to an existing JoyID does not necessarily create a different CKB owner. If JoyID returns an existing account, the list updates that account rather than inventing another user.</p><p>Switching is blocked while the demo is signing, submitting, or settling queued work. Disconnect removes the active connection but keeps remembered account hints and durable work. Saved accounts may require wallet authentication again after reload. External wallet account changes reset the app’s active session before attaching the new owner. The included tests use injected-wallet and popup fixtures; real JoyID passkey creation and device-specific prompts still require manual testing.</p>' },
      { id: 'profile-activity', title: 'Expand activity and logs in your profile', html: '<p>Open the connected Pilot profile and expand <strong>Activity &amp; logs</strong>. Each recorded action shows its human-readable name, app/action IDs, signing method, session, status, timestamp, transaction hash or off-chain target, and an expandable timeline. Queued and signed actions remain distinct from confirmed chain transactions; off-chain receipts say <strong>Accepted by app</strong>.</p><p>The demo records counter, reusable delegation, and social actions into this account-filtered history. It persists across sessions and reloads on the same browser origin. It is local, editable, best-effort diagnostics, not a global history or proof of authorization. Existing legacy inspector records are still available in the separate demo inspector. Session private keys and arbitrary action payloads are excluded from the new history writer.</p>' },
      { id: 'flow-verification', title: 'Verified on CKB testnet', html: '<p>Before shared usernames were introduced, on October 12, 2026, the session flow used an injected EIP-6963 wallet through CCC with real owner signatures, session signatures, broadcasts, and confirmations. One owner signature authorized the selected action. Two moves updated optimistically and confirmed on-chain with zero additional owner signatures. Reload restored the profile, grant, position, and remaining budget. One further owner signature recovered the session capacity.</p><p>Receipts and signature counts are recorded in <code>deployments/pilot-flow-testnet-proof.json</code>. Manual extension-wallet testing remains outstanding. That proof covers the legacy counter manifest and its one action. The new <a href="#cell-delegation">developer delegation flow</a> adds named builders, selected permission subsets, explicit migration, and an owner-signed action path; it has separate local-chain verification.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'usernames', group: 'CORE CONCEPTS', title: 'Usernames & transfers', eyebrow: 'SEND TO A NAME', description: 'One verified identity, one username, one CKB recipient address. Pilot manages usernames across connected apps.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'identity-model', title: 'Register a verified name', html: '<p>Names use 3–20 characters: a letter first, followed by letters, numbers, or underscores. Lookup is case-insensitive and accepts an optional <code>@</code>. A wallet signs a five-minute, single-use challenge that binds the name, network, recipient address, signing identity, and the Pilot service.</p><p>JoyID registrations store the public key of the primary P-256 passkey and verify its WebAuthn signature and address binding. Other wallets may register through supported CCC CKB, EVM, Bitcoin, Nostr, or Dogecoin message signatures. Each name, address, and signing identity is unique within the connected Pilot service. JoyID subkeys and temporary session keys cannot register names in this version.</p><p>Registration does not authorize spending or automatic actions. Usernames are public, off-chain records; uniqueness applies within the connected Pilot service and is not enforced on-chain. Renaming, key rotation, deletion, and linking additional wallets are not implemented.</p>' },
      { id: 'recipient-resolution', title: 'Resolve funds and NFT recipients', html: '<p>In the demo, use <strong>Send with Pilot usernames</strong>. Enter a name, select CKB or Spore NFT, enter an amount or Spore ID, and review the resolved recipient address and fee. <strong>Sign and send</strong> requests a signature from the connected owner wallet.</p><p>Recipient resolution rejects a different network. The CKB builder checks the minimum receiving-cell capacity, and the Spore builder checks that the connected wallet owns the NFT. These transfers are separate from the counter session and do not use its Fly forward grant. Other NFT standards need their own transaction builders.</p><p>Signed transfer bytes are saved before broadcast. An uncertain transfer blocks a new one until reconciled; use <strong>Check / retry saved transfer</strong> to query or resend the same transaction. Receipts persist locally, and a Web Lock prevents concurrent sending from multiple tabs on this origin.</p>', demo: true },
      { id: 'username-sdk', title: 'Username and transfer helpers', html: '<p>Pilot manages wallet-backed usernames. Apps use its username helpers to find an existing profile, register a name, or resolve a recipient. Transfer builders prepare a transaction; your app must show a review and request the owner signature separately.</p>', table: [['findByAddress(address)', 'Find the Pilot profile for a connected wallet address.'], ['register(ownerSigner, username)', 'Register a name using a wallet-signed ownership proof.'], ['resolve(username, network)', 'Resolve a username to its verified recipient address on the requested network.'], ['prepareCkbToUsername()', 'Prepare a CKB transfer to a Pilot username.'], ['prepareSporeToUsername()', 'Prepare a Spore NFT transfer to a Pilot username.']], },
      { id: 'username-verification', title: 'Verification status', html: '<p>Wallet proof verification and CKB/Spore construction passed automated checks on October 13, 2026. Browser checks exercise real registration signatures and transfers against a mocked chain, including persisted receipts and detailed logs. Manual JoyID browser registration and live username-based CKB/NFT transfers still need verification; the earlier session testnet proof does not certify these new flows.</p>' },
      { id: 'username-migration', title: 'Move from browser-local profiles', html: '<p>Previous browser-local usernames are not automatically imported or reserved. Register the name through Pilot with your wallet. If you already have a funded counter session, the modal lets you register your name without funding another session; recovery remains available.</p><p>Transfers expose timestamped signing, broadcast, confirmation, and error logs, reviewed fees, raw transactions, exact signed bytes, and node receipts under <strong>Transaction details and logs</strong>. Their receipt history stays local even though username records are shared.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'sessions', group: 'CORE CONCEPTS', title: 'Sessions & permissions', eyebrow: 'SET THE BOUNDARIES', description: 'A session is an allowance to do specific things. Authority comes from the chain policy.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'session-descriptor', title: 'The session descriptor', html: '<p>The SDK tracks a session ID, network, policy version, and local expiration timestamp. These fields describe a session; they do not grant permission to spend ordinary wallet cells.</p>', ...code("const session = {\n  id: 'my-session',\n  network: 'ckb-testnet',\n  policyVersion: 'counter-v1',\n  expiresAt: Date.now() + 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n};") },
      { id: 'on-chain-authority', title: 'Authorize on-chain', html: '<p><code>authorizeCounter(ownerSigner, options)</code> accepts an existing CCC signer and funds the session cell. Its lock binds the owner lock hash, compressed session public key, maximum per-move fee, and minimum preserved capacity.</p><p>The owner approves setup. Subsequent allowed counter moves use the session key, without invoking the owner signer for each move.</p>' },
      { id: 'limits', title: 'Enforce the allowance', html: '<p>The contract permits exactly one counter increment per move. It rejects extra payments, changed locks, invalid signatures, and overspending. Costs are nonnegative <code>bigint</code> values in integer shannons.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Expiration is currently local.</strong><p>The core rejects new actions after expiry. Previously submitted transactions still need reconciliation. On-chain authority ends through owner recovery or exhausted budget, not the local clock.</p></div>' },
      { id: 'revoke', title: 'End a session', html: '<p><code>revokeCounter(ownerSigner, deployment, sessionCell, currentOutPoint)</code> consumes the session cell using owner authorization. The owner needs an ordinary live cell for the recovery transaction. Finish pending settlement first and use the current session outpoint.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'execution', group: 'CORE CONCEPTS', title: 'Optimistic execution', eyebrow: 'KEEP THE UI MOVING', description: 'Immediate local acceptance. Durable records. Real background settlement.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'prepare', title: 'Prepare the next move', html: '<p><code>prepare()</code> prevalidates up to ten alternative next actions against the same projected state and available budget. This is one set of next moves, not a ten-level search tree. A readiness result is valid only at its current state revision.</p>' },
      { id: 'accept', title: 'Reserve, persist, render', html: '<p><code>execute()</code> serializes acceptance, reserves the full estimated cost, and durably saves the action before exposing optimistic state. Render <code>pilot.state</code> after it resolves. Local acceptance is not a guarantee of chain settlement.</p>' },
      { id: 'settle', title: 'Settle in order', html: '<p>Settlement runs sequentially through the adapter. The default <code>autoSettle</code> is enabled. Use <code>confirmedState</code>, <code>queueLength</code>, and events for reconciliation. Confirmed actions consume their full cost reservation in this version.</p>', ...code("onEvent(event) {\n  if (event.type === 'settled') {\n    console.log(event.transactionHash);\n  }\n  if (event.type === 'reverted') {\n    render(pilot.state);\n  }\n}") },
      { id: 'recover', title: 'Recover without duplicate payments', html: '<p>Unknown failures retain the queue and budget reservations. Call <code>flush()</code> after reconnecting. The adapter must retry the same signed transaction, not build a new payment after a timeout.</p><p>A definitive <code>PermanentSettlementError</code> rolls back the failed action and all dependent queued actions to the last confirmed state. Call <code>invalidate()</code> when external information changes.</p>' },
      { id: 'transaction-inspector', title: 'Inspect the queue and transaction logs', html: '<p>The browser demo renders accepted moves immediately. Its transaction inspector separately shows the live FIFO queue, reserved fees, settlement status, retries, and errors. Expand a transaction to inspect its signing and submission timeline, raw transaction and witnesses, node receipts, actual fee, and confirmation details.</p><p>Local logs survive reloads, and the inspector can export a diagnostic record without session private keys. Queued actions are browser-local; the <a href="#board">Pilot Board</a> combines confirmed on-chain activity, configured shared off-chain receipts, and same-origin account logs. Keeping the game optimistic does not mean that a queued move is already confirmed by the chain.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'integration', group: 'BUILD WITH PILOT', title: 'Connector & React modal', eyebrow: 'BRING PILOT TO YOUR APP', description: 'Use the SDK connector for consent and authorization, and the reusable React modal for the user flow.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'local-packages', title: 'Install Pilot in your app', html: '<p><code>@owl/pilot</code> exports the engine and counter connector. <code>@owl/pilot-react</code> exports the modal. Run the <a href="#quickstart/build-packages">GitHub installer</a> from your existing npm app: it clones Pilot if needed, builds it, and installs the SDK, React 19 modal, and wallet connector automatically. Publishing to npm or GitHub Packages is not required. The installer refreshes its SDK and React dependency copies even when the checkout package version is unchanged, so rerunning after a pull picks up new APIs. The remaining sections explain how to wire the connector and modal after installation.</p>' },
      { id: 'connector-authorization', title: 'Connect and authorize', html: '<p>Supply an existing CCC owner signer and a <code>PilotManifest</code> for the recognized counter deployment. The following runs after the user selects a permission. The host generates and persists the session key before authorization, and durably saves exact signed setup bytes before broadcast so an interruption can resume the same approval.</p>', ...code("import {\n  PilotCounterConnector, assertCounterPermission,\n} from '@owl/pilot';\n\nconst connector = new PilotCounterConnector(ownerSigner, manifest);\nconst account = await connector.getAccount();\n\nconst { cell, grant } = await connector.authorize(selectedPermissions, {\n  sessionPublicKey,\n  onSigned: async (cell, bytes) => saveSignedSession({ cell, bytes }),\n  onTransaction: (event) => recordTransactionEvent(event),\n});\n\n// Check before accepting an action into the Pilot engine.\nassertCounterPermission(manifest, grant, 'counter.increment');"), },
      { id: 'react-modal', title: 'Use the Pilot modal', html: '<p>Import the component and its stylesheet, then provide account, profile, manifest, session, and busy state plus callbacks for owner connection, profile saving, permission authorization, resume, balance refresh, disconnect, and recovery.</p><p>The modal manages its steps, explicit checkbox consent, focus, and permission presentation. The host manages CCC wallet discovery, shared username registration and lookup, session-key custody, the engine, and transaction logs. See <code>apps/demo/src/main.tsx</code> for the counter integration and <code>packages/react/src/PilotModal.tsx</code> for the prop types.</p>', ...code("import { PilotModal } from '@owl/pilot-react';\nimport '@owl/pilot-react/styles.css';") },
      { id: 'modal-activity', title: 'Record actions in the reusable profile', html: '<p>Both modals include <code>PilotActivityLog</code> for a connected address. Call <code>recordPilotActivity(localStorage, entry)</code> when an action is accepted, signed, submitted, retried, or settled. Reuse the same <code>id</code>, <code>appId</code>, and <code>sessionId</code> through the lifecycle. Fields include <code>ownerAddress</code>, <code>actionId</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>status</code>, optional hash/target/fee/grant ID, and a public event message.</p><p>The default browser journal updates the modal live and across tabs. For another store, pass an <code>activity</code> array to either modal or render <code>PilotActivityLog</code> directly. Entries are filtered again against the connected <code>address</code>. The host must wire its action/adapter events: rendering a modal alone does not observe arbitrary signing calls. Never put private keys, sensitive payloads, or authentication secrets in log strings.</p><p><code>PilotOffchainModal</code> also accepts <code>approvedPermissions</code> to present an existing social session as a profile with read-only permissions and history.</p>', ...code("import { recordPilotActivity } from '@owl/pilot';\n\nrecordPilotActivity(localStorage, {\n  id: actionId, ownerAddress: connectedAddress,\n  appId: 'my.app', sessionId: session.id,\n  actionId: 'game.forward', name: 'Move forward',\n  kind: 'onchain', status: 'signed', transactionHash,\n  event: { phase: 'signed', message: 'Saved before broadcast.' },\n});") },
      { id: 'supported-policy', title: 'Integrate a supported chain policy', html: '<p>The legacy counter connector supports <code>counter-v1</code>. The new <a href="#cell-delegation">PilotConnector and PilotDelegationAdapter</a> support developer-defined named actions under the reusable <code>delegation-v1</code> lock and its separately deployed <code>delegation-sponsored-v1</code> variant. See <a href="#paymaster">Owl Paymaster</a> for pool-paid transaction fees. PilotModal supports both manifest versions, independent permission checkboxes, a prepared delegation review, and continuing without automation. Integrating other CKB app actions requires a compatible on-chain policy and adapter that actually restrict the delegated signer. An arbitrary manifest does not make a contract compatible.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'adapters', group: 'BUILD WITH PILOT', title: 'Adapters', eyebrow: 'CONNECT THE CHAIN', description: 'The adapter translates application intent into validated state transitions and chain receipts.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'contract', title: 'Three methods. Clear responsibilities.', table: [['validate(action, context)', 'Check permissions, action validity, and full reserved cost.'], ['apply(state, action)', 'Return the next state with a pure, deterministic transition.'], ['settle(request)', 'Build, sign, broadcast, and wait for confirmation. Return a transaction hash.']] },
      { id: 'settlement', title: 'Make settlement durable', html: '<p>Every settlement request includes a stable <code>id</code>, action, session, and <code>reservedCost</code>. Persist the signed transaction for that ID before broadcasting and reconcile it on retry, including after restart. Never spend beyond the reservation.</p><p>Throw <code>PermanentSettlementError</code> only when the action definitely cannot have settled. Recheck fresh cell availability and actual chain policy before submission.</p>' },
      { id: 'counter-adapter', title: 'The CKB counter adapter', html: '<p><code>CkbCounterAdapter.create()</code> accepts a client, deployment, funded session cell, session descriptor, private session key, and a durable settlement store. It verifies deployment and key identity, signs counter moves, and persists exact transaction bytes before broadcast.</p><p>Its <code>currentOutPoint</code> follows the confirmed session cell for subsequent moves and owner recovery. Each move has its own transaction; parallel settlement and transaction batching are not implemented.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'storage', group: 'BUILD WITH PILOT', title: 'Storage & recovery', eyebrow: 'REMEMBER THE FLIGHT', description: 'Persist accepted actions and signed transactions so interruptions can be reconciled.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'stores', title: 'Choose a journal store', html: '<p><code>MemoryStore</code> is for tests and local simulations. <code>LocalStorageStore</code> persists the SDK journal across browser reloads. Both implement <code>load()</code> and <code>save()</code>.</p>', ...code("import { LocalStorageStore } from '@owl/pilot';\n\nconst store = new LocalStorageStore(\n  window.localStorage,\n  'pilot:my-session',\n);") },
      { id: 'atomic-writes', title: 'Keep writes atomic', html: '<p>A store must replace its journal atomically and leave the previous record intact if saving fails. State and actions must be JSON-serializable. Restored journals are trusted local records, not cryptographic authorization.</p><p>The counter adapter also needs a separate <code>SettlementStore</code> for signed transaction bytes and the current cell outpoint.</p>' },
      { id: 'concurrency', title: 'One active instance per key', html: '<p>The SDK serializes actions within one instance. Multiple browser tabs need an exclusive lease or transactional store. The browser demo uses a Web Lock to prevent two tabs from running the same session.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Session-key custody is separate.</strong><p>The demo keeps its key in localStorage. The prototype does not isolate that key from application JavaScript or provide a production key vault.</p></div>' },
      { id: 'resume', title: 'Resume a saved journal', html: '<p><code>Pilot.create()</code> loads a matching stored session journal and resumes settlement when enabled. A mismatched descriptor is rejected. Keep journals with unresolved transactions so retries can reconcile the original submission.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'api', group: 'REFERENCE', title: 'SDK reference', eyebrow: 'THE TOOLKIT', description: 'The public surface of the current Pilot engine.',
    sections: [
      { id: 'creation', title: 'Pilot.create(options)', html: '<p>Creates or restores an engine. Required options: <code>session</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>budget</code>, <code>adapter</code>, and <code>store</code>. Optional: <code>lookahead</code> (1–10, default 10), <code>autoSettle</code> (default true), <code>now</code>, and <code>onEvent</code>.</p>' },
      { id: 'methods', title: 'Methods', table: [['prepare(candidates)', 'Prevalidate candidate actions for the current state revision.'], ['readiness(id)', 'Return ready, revision, cost, and an optional reason.'], ['execute(id)', 'Persist and accept a prepared action; return its settlement ID.'], ['flush()', 'Drain queued settlement or resume after a transient failure.'], ['invalidate()', 'Advance the revision and discard cached readiness.'], ['reconcileConfirmed(state, remainingBudget)', 'Adopt a verified owner action with an empty queue; cannot increase the session allowance.']] },
      { id: 'state', title: 'State & budget', table: [['state', 'A cloned snapshot of projected, optimistic state.'], ['confirmedState', 'A cloned snapshot of the last confirmed state.'], ['queueLength', 'The number of accepted actions awaiting settlement.'], ['availableBudget', 'Remaining budget minus all pending cost reservations.']] },
      { id: 'events', title: 'Events', table: [['accepted', 'id'], ['settled', 'id, transactionHash'], ['retry', 'id, error'], ['reverted', 'ids, error']], html: '<p>Observer callbacks are isolated: throwing from an event handler cannot interrupt execution or settlement.</p>' },
      { id: 'connector-api', title: 'Connector & permissions', table: [['PilotCounterConnector(owner, manifest)', 'Wrap an existing CCC signer with the supported counter manifest.'], ['connector.getAccount()', 'Read the recommended owner address and balance.'], ['connector.authorize(selected, options)', 'Authorize a funded session; return its cell and manifest-bound grant. Options include sessionPublicKey, onSigned (persist before broadcast), onSubmitted, and onTransaction.'], ['counterManifestHash(manifest)', 'Validate and hash the supported manifest with canonical field ordering.'], ['createCounterGrant(manifest, selected)', 'Require explicit selection of the supported permission and create a grant.'], ['assertCounterPermission(manifest, grant, permission)', 'Reject an unapproved action or a manifest that differs from the approved grant.']], html: '<p>Types include <code>PilotManifest</code> and <code>PilotGrant</code>. <code>PilotModal</code> and <code>PilotProfileView</code> are exported separately from <code>@owl/pilot-react</code>.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'paymaster', group: 'ECOSYSTEM', title: 'Owl Paymaster', eyebrow: 'FUND THE NEXT FLIGHT',
    description: 'A shared home for transaction fees. Deposit test CKB into Owl’s paymaster.',
    app: true,
    sections: [
      { id: 'paymaster-model', title: 'Pilot signs. The paymaster sponsors.', html: '<p>Pilot signs actions with an authorized session key. A paymaster covers the network fee from a funded pool, so the user can interact without paying that fee. The chain still charges a fee; sponsorship changes who pays it.</p><p>The current prototype supports deposits to Owl’s configured public testnet address. The repository now includes a sponsorship service and separate counter-sponsored-v1 and delegation-sponsored-v1 policies. They preserve user app-cell capacity and spend the network fee from a pinned pool input/change pair. The original public v1 deployments remain user-funded; both sponsored code cells were deployed and verified live on public testnet on October 18, 2026. Their outpoints are recorded in deployments/pilot-sponsored.json. Running the pool service and configuring the demo are still required to activate sponsorship.</p>' },
      { id: 'deposit-flow', title: 'Review and sign a deposit', html: '<p>Connect an owner wallet through CCC, enter a test CKB amount, and choose <strong>Review deposit</strong>. The review shows the recipient, deposit amount, actual transaction fee, and total wallet cost before requesting your signature. The form checks the connected network and the receiving lock’s minimum cell capacity.</p><p>This transfer requires an owner-wallet signature; it is not covered by the demo’s Fly forward permission. Automatic refunds are not implemented.</p>' },
      { id: 'paymaster-configuration', title: 'Current configuration and sponsorship status', html: '<p>Owl’s public CKB testnet receiving address is configured in <code>deployments/owl-paymaster.json</code> and displayed above. Connect a wallet to review a deposit. This address requires at least <strong>61 test CKB</strong> per deposit to create a cell, plus the transfer fee. <code>VITE_PAYMASTER_ADDRESS</code> can override the recipient when serving or building the website.</p><p>The original policies only permit one session input and successor. Both new sponsored binaries are now deployed and verified live on public testnet; their verified Deployment objects are recorded in deployments/pilot-sponsored.json. Keep poolLock pinned to the pool address. Next, configure PILOT_PAYMASTER_PRIVATE_KEY on the server and run npm run paymaster. For a new deployment, use npm run paymaster:deploy -- both with a funded deployer. Run npm run paymaster:account to create or reuse a local testnet deployer; its private key is saved only in git-ignored .pilot/sponsored/deployer.json with owner-only file permissions. The deployment script uses that file unless PILOT_DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY is set. The public address is recorded in deployments/sponsored-deployer.json. Fund the deployer separately: the current two binaries occupy about 200,698 CKB combined, so 210,000 test CKB provides room for fees and change. The 100,000 test CKB fee pool remains separate. Deployment creates code cells with substantial operator-funded occupied capacity and is separate from sponsoring user actions. The key must own the configured receiving address; no private key belongs in browser configuration. Set VITE_PILOT_PAYMASTER_URL=https://api.example/api/paymaster in the demo build. Missing deployment configuration blocks sponsored use rather than reverting to user-paid fees. The configured pool balance was verified at 100,000 test CKB on October 18, 2026; the website displays the live balance. A deposit alone does not activate sponsorship.</p>' },
      { id: 'sponsored-accounting', title: 'Who pays what?', table: [['CKB network transaction fee', 'Pool input minus pool change; the user pays zero in sponsored mode.'], ['App cell storage capacity', 'User funds the recoverable occupied capacity. Sponsored flight locks 200 CKB, without depositing a fee budget.'], ['Transfers, NFTs, app payments', 'User provides the asset or payment. Sponsorship only pays the network fee.'], ['Pool deposits', 'An explicit owner-signed funding transfer. The current docs deposit form pays its displayed transfer fee.'], ['Off-chain actions', 'No CKB transaction fee.']], html: '<p>Sponsored transaction logs distinguish the actual quoted pool network fee from the virtual action allowance and state that the user network fee is zero. The SDK independently verifies that the user inputs equal user outputs exactly. The quote cannot change the original inputs, outputs, state, or application witnesses. Both parties sign the same transaction effects. SponsorQuote.id commits the unsigned quoted bytes, including application witnesses; SponsorQuote.transactionHash is the separate CKB transaction hash. Quotes with different type witnesses cannot collide simply because their raw transaction hash matches. The pool service dry-runs all scripts before releasing its signature and saves exact bytes before broadcast.</p><p>The service bounds each fee, per-owner daily spending and total daily spending. Unsigned quotes do not reserve pool cells. The flight demo saves recovery signatures too, blocks further moves during an unresolved recovery, and resumes its original transaction after reload. Unknown submissions retry exact saved bytes; only an explicit server response proving an unsigned quote expired or conflicted permits an automatic-action re-quote. Owner approvals with expired quotes require a fresh review/signature. Empty pools or service failures never trigger a user-paid fallback. Pool balance includes occupied capacity; a 61 CKB cell needs additional fee headroom to sponsor transactions. A single pool cell supports only one pending spend; fund multiple cells for concurrency.</p><p>Flight allowance is decremented on-chain as a virtual action budget, without reducing user capacity. Reusable delegation allowance is an SDK journal limit; its contract enforces transitions, pool identity and per-transaction fee cap, while the pool service enforces durable owner/global daily caps. It does not enforce a cumulative per-session sponsored budget on-chain. Old sessions cannot become sponsored in place: recover and authorize a new sponsored session.</p>' },
      { id: 'paymaster-operator', title: 'Run the fee sponsor', code: `# Server .env: pool operator secret, never VITE_
PILOT_PAYMASTER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...
PILOT_PAYMASTER_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-demo.example
PILOT_PAYMASTER_MAX_FEE=100000
PILOT_PAYMASTER_DAILY_BUDGET=100000000
PILOT_PAYMASTER_OWNER_DAILY_BUDGET=1000000
# Start after deploying sponsored contracts and funding the pool
npm run paymaster
# Demo .env (public URL only), then rebuild
VITE_PILOT_PAYMASTER_URL=https://api.example/api/paymaster`, html: '<p>Serve the backend behind HTTPS. With DATABASE_URL set, instances share a PostgreSQL journal and database row lock for that pool. Without it, standalone servers must share the same persistent PILOT_PAYMASTER_FILE on a filesystem supporting file locks. Vercel always requires PostgreSQL. Do not run independent journals against the same pool key. The startup script only permits testnet and verifies that the configured key owns the pool address. Default allowlists include both known Pilot variants; signed ordinary-owner transactions, including username transfers, are also eligible unless PILOT_PAYMASTER_ALLOW_OWNER_TRANSACTIONS=false. No unsigned transaction can obtain a usable pool signature. The public pool is operator controlled; deposits are not trustless escrow and automatic refunds are not implemented.</p>' },
      { id: 'vercel-hosting', title: 'Deploy the demo and APIs on Vercel', html: '<p>Import the GitHub repository with the project root at the repository root, choose Other and Node.js 22.x, and keep the committed vercel.json configuration. It installs the SDK, React wrapper and demo dependencies, serves apps/demo/dist, and routes /api/pilot, /api/social and /api/paymaster through one Node request handler. Create the docs/landing site as a separate Vercel project rooted at apps/docs; its own vercel.json builds to dist. If Vercel selects Services as the Application Preset, the docs may be assigned the public / route even when Root Directory is already ./. For the demo/backend project, keep ./ and switch Application Preset to Other to use the root configuration; do not import apps/demo alone because its backend function lives in the root api directory. For docs, use Import single project on the docs entry, rooted at apps/docs with preset Vite. Rust contract folders are deployed to CKB, not Vercel. If the demo Root Directory actually says apps/docs, clear that directory and redeploy. Root Directory is configured per Vercel project, not by the repository configuration. The SDK can still be integrated into apps hosted elsewhere.</p><p>Attach PostgreSQL and set a pooled DATABASE_URL, an exact HTTPS PILOT_APP_ORIGIN with no trailing slash, and the server-only PILOT_PAYMASTER_PRIVATE_KEY that owns the funded pool. Set the two public API build URLs below before deploying. The username API uses /api/pilot automatically. Default registry signing origin, social audience and browser allowlists inherit PILOT_APP_ORIGIN. Do not copy localhost allowlist overrides from .env.example into production. A new domain or preview needs matching origins and a separate test database/pool configuration.</p><p>PostgreSQL now stores usernames/challenges, paymaster quotes/signed retry bytes/leases/fee accounting, shared social state/receipts/revocations, and expiring IP rate-limit counters. All instances using a pool must share one database. A row lock serializes journal changes across cold starts; transaction bytes and fee accounting commit before broadcast. Social replay checks, mutation and revocation are atomic. Vercel never falls back to JSON or temporary files, and database failures retain the original-request retry behavior.</p><p>After deployment, check /api/pilot/health, /api/social/manifest and /api/paymaster/status. Then authorize a fresh sponsored session and verify its chain receipt; also publish a social post and reload. Existing sessions retain their fee rules. The root VERCEL.md contains the full deployment and verification guide. Hosted Vercel execution and live sponsored user actions still need operator verification.</p><p>The initial PostgreSQL adapter uses bounded JSONB journal documents and serializes operations per pool/audience. Existing journal and demo receipt caps remain. Lock waits time out after five seconds; the function duration is sixty seconds. This adaptation adds neither a background settlement worker nor automatic JSON migration. Stop old services and explicitly migrate their records before switching a live pool; independent journals against one pool are unsafe. Browser keys and local diagnostics stay in the browser; wallet private keys are not database fields.</p>', ...code(`# Vercel server environment
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://...
PILOT_APP_ORIGIN=https://pilot.example
PILOT_PAYMASTER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...

# Public build variables; never put secrets here
VITE_PILOT_PAYMASTER_URL=https://pilot.example/api/paymaster
VITE_PILOT_SOCIAL_URL=https://pilot.example/api/social`, 'dotenv') },
      { id: 'deposit-recovery', title: 'Track and recover a deposit', html: '<p>The website persists the signed transaction before broadcast. Deposit receipts survive reloads in the same browser. Use <strong>Refresh</strong> to query testnet confirmation or <strong>Retry saved deposit</strong> to reconcile an uncertain submission and resend its original transaction when needed.</p><p>An unresolved deposit prevents another deposit in this browser. Receipt history is local to this website’s origin; it is not a complete record of transfers made elsewhere.</p>' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'board', group: 'ECOSYSTEM', title: 'Pilot Board', eyebrow: 'THE FLIGHT LOG',
    description: 'Explore Pilot activity across apps: on-chain receipts, shared off-chain receipts, and available local logs.',
    app: true,
    sections: [
      { id: 'board-coverage', title: 'Activity across apps and accounts', html: '<p>The Board indexes all four known Pilot lock policies on CKB testnet: counter-v1, delegation-v1, counter-sponsored-v1, and delegation-sponsored-v1. It includes setup and funding, delegated app actions, owner transitions, and recovery across all users and apps using those code hashes. It does not filter by the counter app or a single owner. A custom lock binary with a different code hash needs to be added to the Board policy catalog.</p><p>All activity, On-chain, Off-chain, and Local logs filters separate sources. Expand any record for owner, session, action, receipt, and full details; local records include every stored timeline entry. Search loaded records by app, action, owner, session, hash, block, status, target, or log message. Load older activity to continue through public history; metrics describe loaded records only. A local action and its public receipt are separate records, while chain fees count each transaction once.</p>' },
      { id: 'board-verification', title: 'Receipts, timelines, and coverage', html: '<p>Chain records come from committed node receipts with actual Pilot input or output locks. Fees use all transaction inputs minus outputs; timestamps come from block headers. Session-key and action labels are decoded from witnesses; the Board relies on chain consensus for script validation. Delegated actions expose their action and manifest hashes because arbitrary app names cannot be recovered from a hash alone. Full transaction data and explorer links remain available.</p><p>For shared off-chain history, set <code>VITE_PILOT_SOCIAL_URL=https://api.example/api/social</code> when building or serving the docs. The included verifier exposes public, newest-first <code>GET /api/social/receipts?limit=50&amp;cursor=...</code> pages, so the Board can load beyond the state endpoint’s latest 100 receipts. These are labeled <strong>Accepted by app</strong>: the configured backend reports acceptance, and the Board does not independently verify receipt signatures. This history endpoint permits cross-origin public reads; action and revocation requests keep their app-origin restrictions. Receipt contents, including action payloads, are public.</p><p>The Board reads all available account journals in this origin’s browser storage, across stored apps and owners, and updates on journal events. Queued, signing, retry, and error logs are local diagnostics, not proof of chain confirmation. Private journals on other origins or devices, legacy inspector-only records, and off-chain actions not submitted to the configured shared verifier cannot be discovered globally. No local logs are uploaded. Public source failures show retryable errors while preserving available records.</p>' },
    ],
  },
];
